\section{Compare What Each Description Takes as Input}

Two tools can both generate an assembler without deriving it from the same information. One can start with opcode fields and explicit operand classes; another can try to invert a printing expression to recover a parser. Likewise, a language that can describe an instruction's effect on registers need not say anything about the hardware cycle in which the write occurs. Compare descriptions by the facts they require, the artifacts they produce, and the checks they perform between the two.

Section~6 of the VADL paper distinguishes behavioral, structural, and mixed processor description languages \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. These categories are useful but not exclusive descriptions of entire tool ecosystems. A behavioral model can state what a load returns without a queue or port model. A structural model can declare two memory ports without identifying which decoded encodings use them. A mixed approach may contain both sorts of information, but it matters whether timing is interleaved with instruction definitions or described separately. VADL separates the ISA from its MiA and uses synthesis to assign instruction operations to stages. It also gives software conventions their own view. The following genuine excerpt from Listing~18 declares an empty VADL ABI for the RV32I example:

\begin{verbatim}
application binary interface ILP32 for RV32I { }
\end{verbatim}

The name is not an implementation of the calling convention. The declaration would have to be filled with conventions and other target information as needed; the paper discusses register aliases, calling conventions, stack alignment, and special instruction sequences in Section~3.5. It would be incorrect to infer the argument registers just from the integer instruction encodings.

\section{Different Ways to Describe the Same Processor}

Sail emphasizes architectural semantics. Armstrong and colleagues' \href{https://doi.org/10.1145/3290384}{ISA Semantics for ARMv8-A, RISC-V, and CHERI-MIPS} describes executable ISA models and definitions for proof assistants. A model of which instruction transitions the architecture permits is useful as a reference even when the silicon is deeply pipelined. It does not supply the resource, latency, and control decisions required to generate a particular pipeline. Conversely, a model rendered in a proof assistant does not by itself prove that some implementation refines it.

The LISA work of \href{https://doi.org/10.1145/309847.310101}{Pees and colleagues} describes cycle-accurate models for programmable DSP architectures. The VADL survey discusses the wider LISA tool ecosystem, including generation for compilation and hardware. In \href{https://doi.org/10.1109/EDTC.1995.470354}{Fauth, Van Praet, and Freericks' nML}, instruction descriptions are organized as an attributed grammar, with an action attribute for semantics and structural state described in a skeleton. Both are examples of mixed descriptions, but common output categories do not imply identical source abstractions, timing expressiveness, or generator maturity.

\section{Backend Description Is a Different Job}

LLVM's \href{https://llvm.org/docs/TableGen/}{TableGen} is a record-description and generation infrastructure. LLVM target backends use it for such things as instruction and register records, selection patterns, scheduling data, encoders, and decoders. Those records serve particular consumers. Taken together they need not define an executable specification for all architectural effects. In fact VADL uses TableGen as an output for parts of its LLVM backend, with other generated code and supporting infrastructure doing other work (Sections~4.4--4.5). What serves as the output of one generator can be the source of generation parameters for another.

The \href{https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gccint/Machine-Desc.html}{GCC machine-description manual} instead describes instruction patterns in terms of GCC's register transfer language (RTL), with operand predicates and constraints, assembly templates, and instruction attributes used by GCC. Its RTL refers to the compiler's program representation, not to synthesizable register-transfer-level hardware. A rule matching or splitting a GCC RTL expression serves GCC's code generation; it is not an instruction interpreter. The VADL paper describes an LLVM prototype and names GCC generation as future work in Section~7, not as a measured second backend.

