\section{Audit a Proposed Translation Between Descriptions}

Suppose an architectural reference gives \(x_5=0\) after \texttt{addi x5, x1, 1} with \(x_1=2^{32}-1\). For a proposed compiler rule, check wrapping 32-bit addition and the immediate restriction independently. The following is audit pseudocode, not syntax for any of the languages above.

\begin{verbatim}
if op == wrapping_add32 and -2048 <= imm <= 2047:
    candidate = ADDI(dst, src, imm)
    check(candidate.result == (src.value + imm) mod 2^32)
    return candidate
return NEED_OTHER_SEQUENCE
\end{verbatim}

An immediate of \(1\) yields zero in the stated trace. An immediate of \(2048\) fails the applicability test even when its arithmetic result is well defined. The invariant is that each accepted match is both encodable and equivalent to the source operation over its admitted operands. A model of ADDI alone still says nothing about where a caller leaves the first argument or how many cycles a dependency costs. \Cref{fig:visual-335} shows the additional inputs. The proof-of-concept compiler benchmark in Section~5.4 is evidence for a subset of the VADL-to-LLVM flow, not an evaluation of all these languages against one another.

This audit must use the source IR's semantics, including its rules for overflow, undefined behavior, and observable memory effects. A compiler is not required to preserve a result for a source execution that its own IR declares impossible, but it may not use that latitude to justify an instruction whose defined executions differ. That boundary is separate from whether a target instruction is encodable.

